WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:03.667
What if a bird has all its parts,

2
00:00:03.867 --> 00:00:05.833
but they're in the wrong places?

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.433
That's our first meme.

4
00:00:07.767 --> 00:00:10.033
The second shows a group project:

5
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:12.533
one member contributes almost nothing,

6
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:14.733
yet the team gets it right.

7
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:16.633
Can a model do that too?

8
00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:18.700
We tested one frozen PIP-Net

9
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.400
on twenty bird photos from ten species.

10
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.500
It adds up learned visual clues,

11
00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:26.867
called prototypes.

12
00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:30.300
We swapped head with tail,
or wing with tail,

13
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.733
adding twenty-four pixels of separation.

14
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:38.933
We measured raw evidence
for the true species, not probability.

15
00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:42.800
In photo ten, either swap cut evidence

16
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.300
from about thirty-two to below one.

17
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:49.167
But in photo twelve,

18
00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:53.467
swapping wing and tail raised evidence

19
00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:56.433
from nineteen to twenty-four.

20
00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:59.433
One prototype's contribution
nearly vanished;

21
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.433
others contributed more.

22
00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:05.467
The prediction stayed correct,
with different support.

23
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:07.833
Across twenty photos,

24
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:12.567
the head-tail swap lowered evidence
every time.

25
00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:15.667
The wing-tail swap lowered it in fourteen

26
00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:17.667
and raised it in six.

27
00:01:17.967 --> 00:01:22.433
We also tested sixteen combinations
of four parts

28
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:26.733
using second-order
Shapley-Taylor analysis

29
00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:29.900
to measure how parts work together.

30
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.433
Head-wing interaction scores
usually decreased.

31
00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:38.500
Lower signed scores don't always mean
weaker effects,

32
00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:42.133
and these scores include effects
from other parts.

33
00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:45.633
Moving the whole bird also changed scores.

34
00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:48.667
Position and visible editing
seams matter,

35
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:52.867
so these results describe our edits
to this model.

36
00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:54.433
The key finding:

37
00:01:54.767 --> 00:02:00.000
the same answer can hide
a different explanation.
